eces. It\'s an internal move, so not bad; but it opens up B7, which is an X-square, which can hurt me later.\n\n**(C, 1)**: C-square. If my opponent plays D1, that opens A1. So this is bad.\n\n**(E, 1)**: Edge, but flipping 3 pieces. Not close to corners. Can form a column with the *pieces.\n\n**(F, 1)**: C-square! So no!\n\n**(G, 1)**: A C-square. Dangerous move.\n\n**(G, 2)**: X-square, bad to go here.\n\n**(H, 2)**: C-square. No.\n\n**(H, 3)**: Flips 4. Large flip, edge move. Can create an edge battle.\n\nThen I get back to the A-column. I want to try to be as quiet as possible. A3, A4, A5, and A6 are all "okay" moves. If I take the A-column, the opponent has B3, B4, and B5 available to them.\n\nNow, A3 allows me to flip the opponent\'s * at B3 to @.\nA4 flips a lot, so I\'m not that interested.\n\nLet\'s check C7, is it valid? No! I can\'t take C7. I should ignore the list of options.\n\nNow to come up with one move.\n\nIf I play A3 and flip B3, that creates a block. I think I will use the "Edge creeping" strategy, by taking the edge with minimal impact.\n\nIt all seems volatile.\n\n(B, 6) is in the center of the board.\n\nA3 is better.\n\nI need to see if A3 provokes bad moves. I just want a calm, easy move.\n\n(A, 3) provokes the response A4. This also gives the response A5, which I want! It is a set-up.\n\nSo I need to check the best case.\n\nI now need to see how the opponent plays.\n\n**Reason:** (A, 3) minimizes new options for the opponent. If the opponent takes the adjacent edge square, I have a strong counter-move at A5. This secures the edge. (A, 3) is safe, quiet, and sets up a potential edge trap.\n\n**Answer: (A, 3)**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the mid-game phase. I am playing as \'@\'. The board shows a cluster of pieces in the center-left. The opponent (\'*\') has pieces on the D1 edge and scattered around the center. I have several options on the A-column edge, row 1 edge, and some internal moves.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **Avoid Bad